<h2>The parties and the marks</h2>
<p>The plaintiffs — Jindal Composite Tubes Pvt Ltd, a Delhi company incorporated in 2003, and a sister concern — manufacture non-metallic pipes under the JINDAL family of marks, holding a word mark registration (No. 1018169, Class 17, PVC pipes, territorially limited to Punjab, Haryana and Himachal Pradesh) and a device mark (No. 1165594, Class 17, HDPE/MDPE/PE pipes, unrestricted). Defendant No. 1, sole proprietor of M/s Goyal Polymers of Sunam, Punjab, sells identical goods under GROJINDAL / GRO JINDAL, claiming use since 26 October 2020; Defendant No. 2 is a Jalandhar distributor. The plaintiffs say they discovered the mark in February 2026, issued notice on 12 March 2026 and sued, seeking an interim injunction under Order XXXIX Rules 1 and 2 CPC in a commercial suit.</p>

<h2>The defences, and why they failed</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Defence raised</th><th>The court&rsquo;s prima facie answer</th></tr>
  <tr><td>No territorial jurisdiction: the cause of action lies in Punjab, and the plaintiffs trade there</td><td>Section 134(2) permits suit where the registered proprietor carries on business; a principal-to-principal distributor is not the plaintiff&rsquo;s subordinate office. &ldquo;This Court is prima facie of the view that it has the territorial jurisdiction to entertain the present Suit&rdquo; (para 12).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Honest concurrent use: &ldquo;GRO&rdquo; from Goyal, JINDAL a common surname, Section 35 protects it</td><td>Section 35 shields a person&rsquo;s own name or business name. JINDAL is neither; &ldquo;the adoption of the Impugned Mark by Defendant No. 1 cannot, prima facie, be said to be honest or bona fide&rdquo; (para 21).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pending rectifications against the registrations defeat the claim</td><td>Section 31(1) confers prima facie validity on registration; pendency of rectification does not dilute it at this stage (para 13).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Suppression of the word mark&rsquo;s territorial limitation</td><td>Rejected — the plaint itself disclosed the limitation (para 14).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Damages are quantifiable, so no irreparable harm</td><td>Injury to goodwill from confusingly similar use on identical goods is not adequately compensable in money; the balance of convenience favoured the registered proprietor (paras 22&ndash;24).</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The core of the reasoning</h2>
<p>Applying the Supreme Court&rsquo;s decision in <em>Renaissance Hotel Holdings v. B. Vijaya Sai</em> ((2022) 5 SCC 1), the court found that GROJINDAL wholly incorporates the registered mark and that &ldquo;the word &lsquo;JINDAL&rsquo; is the dominant and essential feature of the Impugned Mark, both visually and phonetically&rdquo; (para 16). With identity of goods — pipes — and identity of trade channels, the triple-identity structure of the infringement analysis under Section 29(2)(b) was prima facie complete. The defendant&rsquo;s offer of a modified style was rejected for a simple reason: if the use itself infringes, cosmetic adjustments do not cure it. All findings were expressly recorded as prima facie (para 27), and the interim injunction restrains use of GROJINDAL / GRO JINDAL or any deceptively similar mark for pipes and fittings — MLC, PVC, UPVC, CPVC, HDPE, MDPE, PE and PPR-C — pending suit.</p>

<h2>Lessons for brand owners and traders</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Wholesale incorporation of a registered word mark is the riskiest form of adoption; prefixes and suffixes rarely displace the registered element as the dominant feature when goods and channels are identical.</p>
  <p>The Section 35 own-name defence is narrow and literal: it protects the trader&rsquo;s actual name or business name, not a surname the trader finds commercially attractive.</p>
  <p>Pending rectification is not a shield at the interlocutory stage — a defendant banking on invalidity must still face Section 31(1)&rsquo;s presumption until the register is actually altered.</p>
  <p>For plaintiffs, pleading hygiene pays: the disclosure of the word mark&rsquo;s territorial limitation in the plaint defused the suppression attack entirely.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This is an informational note on an interlocutory order; the findings are prima facie and the suit remains pending. Nothing here is an opinion on the eventual merits.</div>
